gl: Disable FallbackToSWIfGLES3NotSupported by default

Upstream enabled this feature in M146 as part of deprecating GLES2
(crbug.com/444049511): when an OpenGL ES 3.0 context cannot be created,
the GPU process no longer retries ES 2.0 but gives up on hardware
acceleration entirely, dropping to software compositing with no WebGL
(the automatic SwiftShader fallback is also deprecated). On VideoCore IV
devices (Pi 0-3), whose vc4 driver correctly reports OpenGL ES 2.0 only,
this silently removed all hardware acceleration.

Disable the feature by default, restoring the pre-M146 ES 2.0 fallback.
The feature is only consulted when ES3 context creation fails, so
ES3-capable devices (v3d on Pi 4/5) are unaffected. Google carved
ChromeOS Flex out of the same feature for the same reason (GLES2-only
hardware in the field; see ShouldFallbackToSWIfGLES3NotSupported in this
file). Verified on a Pi 3B: with the feature disabled, the GPU process
falls back to ES 2.0 and runs hardware-accelerated on vc4 via ANGLE.

Can be re-enabled for testing via chrome://flags
(#fallback-to-sw-if-gles3-not-supported) or --enable-features.
--- a/ui/gl/gl_features.cc
+++ b/ui/gl/gl_features.cc
@@ -113,15 +113,12 @@ BASE_FEATURE(kDefaultPassthroughCommandD
 
 // Controls whether the GPU process falls back to software if GLES3 is not
 // supported.
+// Disabled for Raspberry Pi: on GLES2-only VideoCore IV devices (Pi 0-3)
+// this feature replaces hardware ES 2.0 with software rendering. It is only
+// consulted when ES3 context creation fails, so ES3-capable hardware
+// (v3d on Pi 4/5) is unaffected.
 BASE_FEATURE(kFallbackToSWIfGLES3NotSupported,
-#if BUILDFLAG(IS_WIN)
-             // TODO(https://crbug.com/444049511): Currently disabled on
-             // Windows for D3D9 users that are still on ES 2. Enable once
-             // crbug.com/40874754 is fixed, deprecating D3D9 usage.
              base::FEATURE_DISABLED_BY_DEFAULT);
-#else
-             base::FEATURE_ENABLED_BY_DEFAULT);
-#endif
 
 #if BUILDFLAG(IS_WIN)
 // If true, VsyncThreadWin will use the compositor clock
